\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $\Omega$ be a bounded planar simply connected domain, or a bounded convex domain in $\mathbb R^n$ for $n\geq3$. Must every first nonconstant Neumann eigenfunction of its Laplacian attain its global maximum and minimum on the boundary rather than in the interior? This is the stated eigenfunction form of the hot-spots question.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{http://aimpl.org/shapesurface/3/}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $\Omega$ be a bounded planar simply connected domain, or a bounded convex domain in $\mathbb R^n$ for $n\geq3$. Must every first nonconstant Neumann eigenfunction of its Laplacian attain its global maximum and minimum on the boundary rather than in the interior? This is the stated eigenfunction form of the hot-spots question.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 20000496 (AIM-ANALYSIS-0028). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
